## Hints for Future Agents
"Add X to a workflow" is not satisfied by leaving the old text on the page while
rewriting the frame around it so the old step reads as untrustworthy. Additive
means the existing step keeps its authority. Watch for this when the natural
motivation for the new step is a weakness in the old one.

The load-bearing distinction that survived the rewrite: codex does not share the
orchestrator's context, so it is a different source of signal. Do not
"simplify" that into a generic "also ask codex" line.

The closing stop condition ends "until codex has run out of alternatives worth
taking" rather than "failed to land a hit" (the first draft), because the user
asked for alternative perspectives, not only refutation.

Kept the file's existing voice, which uses contractions ("Don't stop", "it's"),
rather than the expanded-contraction rule from rules/reporting.md. That rule
governs reported work and technical documents, not this prompt text, and
rules/engineering.md says to follow the convention already in place.

The codex guidance here intentionally restates parts of rules/engineering.md's
Codex section (threadId continuation, one topic per thread, paste the real code,
bring back what changed your mind). The duplication is deliberate: the skill has
to stand on its own when invoked, but if that rule changes, this file should be
updated to match.

Version bump was PATCH, not MINOR: this refines an existing skill rather than
adding a new one.

## Codebase Discoveries
plugins/abatilo-core/CLAUDE.md carries a mandatory version-bump rule with a
change-type-to-increment table; .claude-plugin/plugin.json is the single source
of truth for the version.

This repo has notes rewriting enabled: `git commit --amend` carried the old note
forward to the new SHA on its own, so re-attaching needs `git notes add -f`
rather than a plain add, which fails with "Found existing notes for object".

